# Deploy Workflow

## What yui needs

The real ship contract is the **car loop** (voice-note an agent → change
on the phone → yui knows it is live). Full requirements vs mechanisms:
[docs/live-change.md](../docs/live-change.md).

Operationally today:

1. **New code reaches the live site.** A change that affects served files
   must land on `master` (direct push or merged PR). That triggers GitHub
   Actions → rsync → https://fuseki.net/voice-wei/. Reload to load it.
2. **Yui gets a signal that it is live.** The current mechanism is an
   **incrementing** header build id (not a SHA — yui glances and compares).
   `?v=N` cache-busts assets so the reload actually shows the new code.
   Other signals (agent confirmation after Actions succeeds, SMS/Signal,
   etc.) are valid and may replace or outrank the header later.

Docs/rules-only pushes are skipped by Actions (`paths-ignore`); they never
change the live site.

## The two invariants (yui, 2026-07-25)

1. **Nothing mei produces ever waits on yui's review.** Yui must never see a
   review request, a "ready for review" state, or an open PR they did not
   explicitly ask for. Branches and PRs are tolerated as platform plumbing
   ONLY when they self-resolve within the same turn.
2. **"Deploy" said in chat is a complete instruction.** No follow-up
   questions, no checkboxes, no waiting.

## Ship protocol (the only one)

1. Work wherever the platform demands (cloud-assigned `cursor/*` branch or
   `master` directly — either is fine).
2. Before ending ANY response in which code changed: fast-forward everything
   into `master`, push `master` once, delete the work branch, and confirm any
   platform PR shows MERGED — never open, never review-requested.
3. PRs, when the platform requires them, stay **drafts**; never assign
   reviewers, never mark ready-for-review, never post review requests.
4. **When yui says "deploy" / "ship" / "release":** run the ~13s local gate
   (or the owning suite if the full gate just ran), land on `master`, push
   once, watch the ~10s deploy job verify the live `VERSION`, and report the
   live version. That is the entire procedure.
5. End-of-turn invariant: `gh pr list --state open` empty, no `cursor/*`
   branches on origin. An open PR or stray branch at end of turn is a failed
   ship.

### Exceptions

- If yui explicitly asks for a PR to review, leave exactly that PR open and
  say so plainly; that is the only source of a review box.
- If mei are genuinely uncertain about a change, ask before pushing to master.

## Version bump

Run `./bump-version.sh` **once per user-facing ship**, in the **same push** as
the change. Never push a bump-only commit: it still deploys new `?v=` / header
numbers with no real code change, which misleads yui about what "new" means
and races other agents.

```
work, commit…  →  ./bump-version.sh  →  commit version files with the change  →  push master once  →  deploy live as vN
                                                                                                      →  reload; header shows vN
```

**Skip the bump** when the push only touches rsync-excluded paths (docs,
tests, `.cursor/`, scripts, etc.).

**Concurrent agents:** before the ship push, `git fetch origin master` and
rebase (or reset+cherry-pick onto origin). If `VERSION` moved, re-run
`./bump-version.sh` so the ship is exactly one past origin, then push once
with content + version together.

## Pipeline (reference)

Deploy-first (yui's direction, 2026-07-25; single-user site): push to
`master` (when not paths-ignored) → rsync `--delete` immediately (excludes
docs/tests/tooling; list shared with `deploy.sh`) → live in ~15s, verified
against the shipped `VERSION`. A parallel `validate` job runs typecheck,
lint, and the full `npm test` gate AFTER the site is live; a red run means
fix forward now, not roll back the ceremony. Agents still run the ~13s gate
(or the owning suite) locally before pushing — CI validation is the backstop,
not the primary check. Manual: `./deploy.sh`. Details:
[docs/setup.md](docs/setup.md).
